WEBVTT

00:00:00.204 --> 00:00:03.354
One image cannot tell you how far away a
point is.

00:00:03.354 --> 00:00:05.554
It could be anywhere along this ray.

00:00:05.554 --> 00:00:08.966
But a second camera sees that whole ray as
a line.

00:00:08.966 --> 00:00:12.641
So the match of a point must lie on one
line in the other image:

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:14.366
its epipolar line.

00:00:14.366 --> 00:00:17.985
The two camera centres and the point form
the epipolar plane,

00:00:17.986 --> 00:00:21.130
and that plane cuts each image in one of
these lines.

00:00:21.095 --> 00:00:26.145
Three vectors in the epipolar plane are
coplanar, so their triple product is zero.

00:00:26.145 --> 00:00:28.908
Written with the cross product as a skew
matrix,

00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:31.270
that one fact is the epipolar constraint,

00:00:31.271 --> 00:00:34.322
and the matrix in the middle is the
essential matrix.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.545
It has rank two, and five degrees of
freedom.

00:00:37.545 --> 00:00:40.007
Scaling t does not change the constraint,

00:00:40.008 --> 00:00:44.271
so two views give the direction of the
translation, but not its length.

00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:45.730
Take the simplest pair:

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.192
no rotation, one step to the right.

00:00:48.192 --> 00:00:53.205
The point one, two, four lands at point
two five, point five in the first image.

00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:56.692
In the second image it lands at point
five, point five.

00:00:56.692 --> 00:01:01.192
Multiply by E, and the epipolar line is y
equals point five.

00:01:01.192 --> 00:01:03.542
The constraint gives exactly zero.

00:01:03.576 --> 00:01:07.526
Now move the point twice as far along the
same ray, to two, four, eight.

00:01:07.526 --> 00:01:09.151
Where does camera two see it?

00:01:09.151 --> 00:01:10.439
Does it leave the line?

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:12.001
Pause and work it out.

00:01:13.496 --> 00:01:17.096
Camera two sees it at point three seven
five, point five:

00:01:17.096 --> 00:01:18.471
still on the line.

00:01:18.471 --> 00:01:22.534
Depth slides a match along its epipolar
line, but never off it.

00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:26.467
In pixels, the same constraint uses the
fundamental matrix,

00:01:26.468 --> 00:01:29.135
which folds in both cameras' intrinsics.

00:01:29.134 --> 00:01:31.671
Each match gives one linear equation in F.

00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:35.230
Eight matches fix it, and the last step
forces rank two,

00:01:35.231 --> 00:01:37.772
so that every line meets at one epipole.
